{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target's defense relies on 'verisimilitude' (truth-likeness) to justify preferring corroborated theories without induction, but this concept was formally shown by David Miller and Tichý in 1974 to be undefined within Popper's own deductive framework unless one assumes a probabilistic distribution of truth over logical content. Since the target asserts the preference is defensible 'on Popper's own terms' while ignoring that Popper's specific definition of verisimilitude collapsed mathematically without smuggling in inductive assumptions, the claim that the preference avoids induction is false.","premise":"Popper's formal definition of verisimilitude fails to distinguish between truth and falsity in comparative assessments without invoking inductive or probabilistic assumptions, as demonstrated by the Miller-Tichý objection.","premises_essential":["5f8544e48088da3dbf5a97f5348c06bd4662fbe5cd67a2a851fbe6dbd935b3c2"],"successor_question":"Given the collapse of Popper's quantitative definition of verisimilitude, can a purely qualitative notion of 'corroboration as a guide to action' be formulated that strictly avoids the principle of the uniformity of nature?","target":"5f8544e48088da3dbf5a97f5348c06bd4662fbe5cd67a2a851fbe6dbd935b3c2"}]}